{"name":"Dwarf Rabbit","hex":"#c8ac89","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dwarf rabbit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small-sized variety of the domestic rabbit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dwarf_rabbit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dwarf rabbit","blocks":[{"text":"Dwarf rabbit refers either (formally) to a rabbit with the dwarfing gene, or (informally) to any small breed of domestic rabbit or specimen thereof, or (colloquially) to any small rabbit. Dwarfism is a genetic condition that may occur in humans and in many animals, including rabbits. True dwarfism is often associated with a cluster of physical abnormalities, including pituitary dwarfism. The process of dwarfing is used to selectively breed for smaller stature with each generation. Small stature is a characteristic of neoteny, which may account for the attraction of dwarf animals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Netherlandwarf.jpg/330px-Netherlandwarf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwarf_rabbit","wikidata":"Q244190"},{"title":"Rabbit","blocks":[{"text":"Rabbits, or bunnies, are small mammals in the family Leporidae, which is in the order Lagomorpha. They are familiar throughout the world as a small herbivore, a prey animal, a domesticated form of livestock, and a pet, having a widespread effect on ecologies and cultures. The most widespread rabbit genera are Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus. The former, Oryctolagus, includes the European rabbit, Oryctolagus cuniculus, which is the ancestor of the hundreds of breeds of domestic rabbit and has been introduced on every continent except Antarctica. The latter, Sylvilagus, includes over 13 wild rabbit species, among them the cottontails and tapetis. Wild rabbits not included in Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus include several species of limited distribution, including the pygmy rabbit, volcano rabbit, and Sumatran striped rabbit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg/330px-Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit","wikidata":"Q9394"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Jeunes_satin_ivoire_yeux_bleus.JPG/500px-Jeunes_satin_ivoire_yeux_bleus.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Jeunes_satin_ivoire_yeux_bleus.JPG/500px-Jeunes_satin_ivoire_yeux_bleus.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Baby Mini Satin rabbitsBlue-eyed white - Satin coat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJeunes_satin_ivoire_yeux_bleus.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/BRACHYLAGUS_IDAHOENSIS.jpg/250px-BRACHYLAGUS_IDAHOENSIS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/BRACHYLAGUS_IDAHOENSIS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/BRACHYLAGUS_IDAHOENSIS.jpg/250px-BRACHYLAGUS_IDAHOENSIS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pygmy rabbit(Sylvilagus idahoensis)375–500 grams (0.827–1.102 lb)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABRACHYLAGUS_IDAHOENSIS.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Ro_pool_gilberte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Ro_pool_gilberte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARo_pool_gilberte.jpg"},{"srcset"